SUPPLY CHAIN MORNING
Issue 301 | Friday, February 27, 2026

Good morning. It is Friday; we will keep it short.

1) Two more carriers say they will stay around the Cape through Q2
Both cited insurance pricing rather than security. War-risk premiums for Red Sea transits are quoted at 0.7% of hull value per voyage, which on a 15,000 TEU ship is more than the fuel saved by not going around Africa. The Suez Canal Authority extended its 15% transit discount again; nobody is taking it.

2) Savannah opens Berth 1 at Ocean Terminal
The renovated berth adds capacity for two 16,000 TEU ships at once. Savannah has been the fastest-growing major US gateway for six years, and the terminal now handles more than 6 million TEU a year. The bottleneck has moved inland to the rail ramps, which is where it always moves.

3) ISM manufacturing 50.4, first reading above 50 since October
New orders rose to 52.1; supplier deliveries slowed slightly, which in this index means demand is picking up. Prices paid climbed to 58, driven by copper and steel. One respondent from a food processor: "Customers are ordering closer to need. Our forecast error has never been higher."

NUMBER OF THE DAY: $4.02. The national average diesel price, down eleven cents from a month ago and the lowest since 2021.

Have a good weekend. The Weekend Read lands tomorrow.

